course: dictionary
lesson: 4625
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Hike can be a noun (a thing, like a long walk) or a verb (an action, like walking a long way). Context tells you which one. «We went on a long hike through the hills.» Here a hike is something you go on, so it is a noun.
explain: «We hiked for six miles before lunch.» Here hiked is something you do, so it is a verb meaning to walk a long way. Try your meaning in the sentence: "We walked for six miles before lunch" still makes sense, so hiked means walked far.
explain: Hike has other meanings too. «The price of milk had a big hike» means a sudden rise. «He hiked up his socks» means pulled up. Read the words around it: price tells you the meaning is about money, and socks tells you it is about pulling something higher.
fact: Hike is a noun and a verb: a hike is a long walk, and to hike is to go on one.
fact: Hikes are often in hills, woods or mountains, and people go for fun or exercise.
fact: «Making noise on a hike helps bears hear you coming and move away.»
fact: On a hike, take water, snacks, a map and good shoes, and check the trail markers at every junction.
fact: Hike can also mean a big sudden rise, as in a price hike, or to pull something up, as in hiking up your socks.
exercise: «We went on a long hike through the woods.» What is a hike here? | A type of bird | *A long walk | A rise in prices | A bag for food
exercise: «The price of bread had a big hike this year.» What does hike mean here? | A pull on the socks | *A sudden rise | A quiet sound | A long walk in the hills
exercise: «We hiked up the hill before lunch.» Find and copy the word that shows hike is a verb here. | *hiked | lunch | hill | before
test: «Making noise on a hike helps bears hear you coming.» What is a hike in this sentence? | A loud shout | *A walk in nature, often in hills or woods | A big rise in price | A kind of bear
test: «She hiked up her socks and ran on.» What does hiked up mean? | Took off | *Pulled up | Lost | Washed
exam: «On a hike, check the trail markers at every junction.» Why should you check them? | *So you stay on the right path and can find your way back | So you can sleep longer | So the bears can hear you | So the price goes down
